<description>
Slide closed foreground objects upward and open foreground objects downward, keeping their columns and stacking on collision.

Input structure
- The grid has a single background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.
- Some objects are closed frames with at least one enclosed background hole inside their bounding box.
- The remaining objects are open line-like sprites with no enclosed hole.

Rule
- Redraw every object on an empty background canvas without changing its color, shape, or horizontal position.
- Closed frame objects slide vertically upward to the first non-colliding position.
- Open objects slide vertically downward to the first non-colliding position.
- Within each direction, preserve the original vertical order so overlapping columns stack instead of crossing.

Key insight
- The only role distinction is whether an object encloses a hole: hole objects pack against the top edge, while non-hole objects pack against the bottom edge.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects, classified by whether their bbox encloses a background hole",
  "path": "closed objects slide straight upward in their original columns; no-hole objects slide straight downward in their original columns, stopping at the edge or the nearest settled object",
  "trajectory": "move each whole object without changing its shape or color",
  "transform": "redraw the settled objects on an empty background canvas, erasing their original positions",
  "multi_step": "settle upward movers from top to bottom and downward movers from bottom to top so same-column overlaps stack without crossing"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 13-17 rows x 13-17 cols, bg=0
  objects:
    total: 4-7, with 2-3 closed + 2-4 open; each type >= 1
    colors: 3-4 distinct foreground colors; a color may appear on 1-2 objects of mixed type
    closed_frames:
      shape: rectangular hollow frame -- all border cells filled, all interior cells empty
      bbox: 3-5 x 3-5; smallest 3x3
    open_crosses:
      shape: axis-aligned cross -- exactly one full row and one full column intersecting
      bbox: 3-5 x 3-5; arm lengths 1-3 from center, at least one arm >= 1 in each direction
    placement: no pixel overlap; objects may touch grid borders; min gap >= 1 cell (Manhattan)
  rule: |
    Closed frames slide UP keeping column position, packing against top edge / earlier closed objects.
    Open crosses slide DOWN keeping column position, packing against bottom edge / earlier open objects.
    Within each group, process in original vertical order (top-first for closed, bottom-first for open)
    so column-overlapping objects stack without crossing.
  output: same grid size; objects preserve shape/color/column; only row changes.
  feasibility: after packing, closed-zone bottom row < open-zone top row (>= 3 row gap observed)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose grid H x W in [13,17].
    2. Choose n_closed in [2,3], n_open in [2,4], total in [4,7].
    3. Generate closed rectangular frames (3-5 x 3-5) and open cross sprites (3-5 x 3-5, random arm lengths 1-3).
    4. Assign 3-4 foreground colors; some colors may be shared across 1-2 objects.
    5. Pack closed frames at top of grid (row 0 onward, respecting column overlaps) and open crosses at bottom (row H-1 upward). Ensure a gap >= 3 empty rows between the two zones. This is the OUTPUT.
    6. Derive INPUT by scattering each object to a random row in the middle region, keeping its column fixed. Ensure no pixel overlaps.
```
